From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 16:01:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 161/225] net: wireless: sc23xx: fix RX BA reorder timeout
 off-by-one

The reorder release timer force-advances the window past a stalled hole
with reorder_buf_advance(count, force=true), where count is the offset of
the highest buffered frame. That loop stops on the highest frame's slot,
so seq_start ends up equal to the last delivered sequence number rather
than one past it -- unlike the in-order path, whose force=false pass does
one extra iteration into the empty slot and breaks.

The result is a window that lags by one forever: after each timeout every
subsequent in-sequence frame arrives at offset 1, gets buffered instead of
delivered, and is only released by the next 100ms timeout. Symptoms were a
flat ~100ms of added RX latency (ping RTT ~122ms vs ~12ms link) and a
"timeout, some RX frames lost" warning on every single received packet
despite perfectly sequential sequence numbers and zero real loss.

Step seq_start over the highest delivered frame with a force=false advance
after the dump so the next in-sequence frame is treated as in-order. This
lets the window self-correct after a single timeout regardless of how it
first fell behind.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/reorder.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/reorder.c b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/reorder.c
index f4dca675b..8fc5af9a6 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/reorder.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/reorder.c
@@ -145,6 +145,18 @@ static void reorder_timeout(struct timer_list *t)
 	wiphy_warn(r->sdev->wiphy, "timeout, some RX frames lost\n");
 	reorder_buf_advance(r, count, true, &deliver);
 
+	/*
+	 * The force-advance above stops on the highest buffered frame's slot, so
+	 * seq_start ends up equal to the last delivered seq, not one past it.
+	 * Step over it into the (now empty) next slot so the next in-sequence
+	 * frame is treated as in-order. The force=false pass does exactly one
+	 * iteration here and breaks on the empty slot. Without this, seq_start
+	 * stays one behind forever: every subsequent frame lands at offset 1,
+	 * gets buffered, and is only released by the next 100ms timeout ->
+	 * permanent ~100ms RX latency and a "frames lost" warning per packet.
+	 */
+	reorder_buf_advance(r, r->win_size, false, &deliver);
+
 out:
 	spin_unlock_irq(&r->sdev->sta_lock);
 
